    content: 'Volcanoes are openings in the Earth\'s surface where hot melted rock, called magma, can escape. When magma reaches the surface, it is called lava. Volcanic eruptions can be both beautiful and terrifying.\n\nUnder the Earth\'s crust, temperatures are extremely high. The heat melts rock into magma. When pressure builds up, the magma pushes through cracks in the surface. This is how a volcanic eruption happens. Some eruptions are gentle, with lava flowing slowly. Others are explosive and can throw ash high into the sky.\n\nThere are about 1,500 active volcanoes on Earth. The Ring of Fire, an area around the Pacific Ocean, contains the most volcanoes. Mount St. Helens in Washington State erupted in 1980, destroying forests and buildings for miles around.\n\nVolcanoes are not only destructive. The ash they produce makes soil very fertile, which is great for farming. Many people live near volcanoes because the land is so rich. Scientists called volcanologists study volcanoes to help predict eruptions and keep people safe.',
